ndraising edge ($5.1M vs $265K)", "Expert ratings cluster at Likely/Solid D", "Prediction markets price Gillen near 90%", "Modest D-leaning district with incumbent advantage"], "rationale": "Gillen holds a dominant money advantage, incumbency, and a district that leans Democratic, while raters and markets uniformly favor her by a wide margin. The AFL-CIO snub and GOP attacks on the 9/11 health vote are minor irritants that don't offset these structural advantages.", "watch": "Any late-breaking scandal or a strong Republican wave environment on Long Island could tighten this race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Laura Gillen (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Laura Gillen defends a Long Island district that leans slightly Democratic, facing Republican Jeanine Driscoll, who won her primary in June. Gillen has raised over $5 million to Driscoll's roughly $265,000, giving her an overwhelming financial edge heading into the fall. Expert raters broadly place the race in the Likely or Solid Democratic column, though a couple call it Lean or Tilt D, and prediction markets price Gillen's chances around 90 percent.\n\nThe race has drawn some local friction: an AFL-CIO endorsement snub, GOP criticism of her vote on a 9/11 health program, and attention as one of New York's swing seats. Still, with no public general-election polling, the evidence rests on fundamentals, money, and ratings, all of which point to a Gillen win with some residual uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9622, "margin": 11.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9401, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9422, "margin": 10.5}]}